The '''2008 North Carolina gubernatorial election''' was held on November 4, 2008, coinciding with the [[2008 United States presidential election|presidential]], [[2008 United States Senate election in North Carolina|U.S. Senate]], [[2008 United States House of Representatives elections in North Carolina|U.S. House]], [[2008 North Carolina Council of State elections|Council of State]] and [[2008 North Carolina judicial election|statewide judicial elections]]. Democrat [[Bev Perdue]] won the election.<ref name=govelect>{{cite news |title=Democrat Perdue becomes NC's 1st female governor |author=Gary Robertson |date=2008-11-04 |agency=[[Associated Press]]}}</ref> With a margin of 3.39%, this election was the closest race of the [[2008 United States gubernatorial elections|2008 gubernatorial election cycle]]. This was the first time that the same party that was elected governor won the state in presidential election as well since 1988. This was the first time Democrats did so since 1976.

Because incumbent Governor [[Mike Easley]] was [[Term limit|term-limited]], the open-seat race was contested between Democrat [[Bev Perdue]], Republican [[Pat McCrory]], and Libertarian [[Michael Munger]]. Likewise, Democrat [[Walter Dalton]], Republican [[Robert Pittenger]], and Libertarian Phillip Rhodes vied to replace term-limited Lieutenant Governor Perdue.

==Primaries==
*May 6, 2008 – Primary elections<ref name="sboe">{{cite web|url=http://www.sboe.state.nc.us/content.aspx?id=72|title=North Carolina State Board of Elections Calendar|publisher=North Carolina State Board of Elections|access-date=2008-11-29|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100618211919/http://www.sboe.state.nc.us/content.aspx?id=72|archive-date=2010-06-18}}</ref>
*Oct. 10, 2008 – Last day to register to vote in general election<ref name="sboe"/>
*Oct. 16 – Nov. 1, 2008 – "One Stop" registration and early voting<ref name="sboe"/>
*November 4, 2008 – General election<ref name="sboe"/>

Candidates Richard Moore, Dennis Nielsen, Robert Orr, and Bev Perdue took part in a forum on January 26, sponsored by the state chapter of the [[NAACP]].<ref name="wral1">{{cite web|url=https://www.wral.com/news/state/story/2359355/|title=Four N.C. Governor's Candidates Answer NAACP Questions in Durham|date=January 28, 2008|publisher=Capitol Broadcasting Company, Inc.|access-date=2008-11-29}}</ref>

The statewide syndicated TV program ''NC Spin'' held debates for both parties' candidates in April.<ref name="nando1">{{cite web|url=http://projects.newsobserver.com/under_the_dome/n_c_spin_debates_set|title=N.C. Spin debates set|date=April 3, 2008|publisher=The News & Observer Publishing Company|access-date=2008-11-29|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20081024212358/http://projects.newsobserver.com/under_the_dome/n_c_spin_debates_set|archive-date=October 24, 2008}}</ref>

===Democratic===

====Candidates====
* [[Richard H. Moore|Richard Moore]], [[North Carolina State Treasurer|state treasurer]]<ref name="nando2">{{cite web|url=http://www.newsobserver.com/front/story/576689.html |title=Moore: "We need a fresh approach." |last=Beckwith |first=Ryan Teague |date=May 22, 2007 |publisher=The News & Observer Publishing Company |access-date=2008-11-29 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080918133340/http://www.newsobserver.com/front/story/576689.html |archive-date=September 18, 2008 }}</ref>
* [[Dennis Nielsen]], retired [[USAF]] colonel<ref name="nando3">{{cite web|url=http://www.newsobserver.com/politics/story/1035420.html |title=Nielsen has principles but lacks staff and cash |last=Bonner |first=Lynn |date=Apr 13, 2008 |publisher=The News & Observer Publishing Company |access-date=2008-11-29 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080918134123/http://www.newsobserver.com/politics/story/1035420.html |archive-date=September 18, 2008 }}</ref>
* [[Bev Perdue]], [[lieutenant governor of North Carolina]]<ref name="nando4">{{cite web|url=http://www.newsobserver.com/news/story/722630.html |title=Perdue announces bid for governor |last=Johnson |first=Mark |date=October 1, 2007 |publisher=The News & Observer Publishing Company |access-date=2008-11-29 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20081006035544/http://www.newsobserver.com/news/story/722630.html |archive-date=October 6, 2008 }}</ref>

====Campaign====
Moore and Nielsen appeared on the edition of ''NC Spin'' broadcast April 13 on most stations, but Perdue declined the invitation to participate.<ref name="DemHopefuls">{{cite web|url=http://www.ncpolicywatch.com/cms/2008/04/09/dem-hopefuls-argue-issues-criticize-absent-opponent/|title=Dem hopefuls argue issues, criticize absent opponent|last=Johnson|first=Mark|date=April 9, 2008|publisher=[[NC Policy Watch]]|access-date=2008-11-29}}</ref> Perdue and Moore met for their final pre-primary debate at [[WRAL-TV]], which was broadcast on several stations across the state on April 22.<ref name="nado5">{{cite web|url=http://www.newsobserver.com/politics/story/1046891.html |title=Perdue, Moore hold civil debate |author=Mark Johnson and Ben Niolet |date=April 23, 2008 |publisher=The News & Observer Publishing Company |access-date=2008-11-29 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080918134511/http://www.newsobserver.com/politics/story/1046891.html |archive-date=September 18, 2008 }}</ref>

On May 6, 2008, Perdue won the Democratic nomination for governor, defeating State Treasurer Moore and Nielsen.<ref name="results1">{{cite web|url=http://results.enr.clarityelections.com/NC/1875/4822/en/md.html?cid=108|title=North Carolina State Board of Elections|date=May 27, 2008|access-date=2008-11-29}}</ref>

