====Campaign====
The Raleigh ''[[News & Observer]]'' reported on January 9, 2008, that McCrory had filed the necessary paperwork with the State Board of Elections to run for governor.<ref name="McCroryFilesPaperwork">{{cite web|url=http://projects.newsobserver.com/under_the_dome/mccrory_files_campaign_paperwork|title=McCrory files campaign paperwork|last=Beckwith|first=Ryan Teague|date=January 9, 2008|publisher=The News & Observer Publishing Company|access-date=2008-11-29|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20081025031125/http://projects.newsobserver.com/under_the_dome/mccrory_files_campaign_paperwork|archive-date=October 25, 2008}}</ref> He announced that he was running in his hometown of Jamestown on January 15, 2008.<ref name="McCroryAnnouncesRun">{{cite web|url=http://www.newsobserver.com/politics/story/882030.html |title=McCrory announces run for N.C. governor |last=Ingram |first=David |date=January 15, 2008 |publisher=The News & Observer Publishing Company |access-date=2008-11-29 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080921194103/http://www.newsobserver.com/politics/story/882030.html |archive-date=September 21, 2008 }}</ref>

====Debates====
Republican candidates Graham, Orr, and Smith held their first debate on October 20, 2007, at [[High Point University]].<ref>[http://www.newsobserver.com/politics/story/744259.html newsobserver.com | Debate is first for GOP candidates for governor<!-- Bot generated title -->] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20071022171146/http://www.newsobserver.com/politics/story/744259.html |date=2007-10-22 }}</ref> The two Democratic candidates held their first debate at the annual conference of the N.C. School Boards Association on November 6, 2007, which hosted a Republican candidates' debate the same day.<ref>[http://projects.newsobserver.com/blogs/perdue_moore_to_debate Perdue, Moore to debate | newsobserver.com projects<!-- Bot generated title -->] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20081024141109/http://projects.newsobserver.com/blogs/perdue_moore_to_debate |date=2008-10-24 }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.newsobserver.com/1565/story/762356.html |title=Mattress could be missing girl's - Crime/Safety - NewsObserver.com |website=www.newsobserver.com |access-date=6 June 2022 |archive-url=https://archive.today/20120904080848/http://www.newsobserver.com/1565/story/762356.html |archive-date=4 September 2012 |url-status=dead}}</ref>

[[UNC-TV]] invited the three announced Republican candidates and two announced Democratic candidates to participate in the campaign's first debates (officially called "forums") to air on statewide television. Each forum is intended to focus on a single topic: on Jan. 10, the state's economy; on February 7, health care; and on April 24, education.<ref>[http://projects.newsobserver.com/blogs/unc_tv_schedules_gubernatorial_forums UNC-TV schedules gubernatorial forums | newsobserver.com projects<!-- Bot generated title -->] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20081023223915/http://projects.newsobserver.com/blogs/unc_tv_schedules_gubernatorial_forums |date=2008-10-23 }}</ref> Video of the forums is available on the UNC-TV website.<ref>[https://web.archive.org/web/20080423093345/http://www.unctv.org/gubernatorial/index.html UNC-TV website]</ref>

The Republican candidates, now joined by new challenger [[Pat McCrory]], debated on [[WRAL-TV]] on January 17. The debate was also broadcast on stations in Charlotte and Wilmington.<ref>[http://www.newsobserver.com/politics/story/888697.html newsobserver.com | Candidates try to show GOP can-do<!-- Bot generated title -->] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080918132638/http://www.newsobserver.com/politics/story/888697.html |date=2008-09-18 }}</ref>

McCrory, Orr, Graham, and Smith met in a televised debate held by [[WTVI]] in Charlotte on April 3. Media accounts said that McCrory was the primary target of attacks by his rivals.<ref>[http://www.newsobserver.com/politics/story/1024505.html "Rivals rip McCrory in GOP debate"] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080415143651/http://www.newsobserver.com/politics/story/1024505.html |date=2008-04-15 }}</ref> The same was true at another [[WRAL-TV]] debate, held on April 15.<ref>[http://www.newsobserver.com/politics/story/1038735.html AP: "McCrory a target at debate"] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080918132924/http://www.newsobserver.com/politics/story/1038735.html |date=2008-09-18 }}</ref>

The final Republican debate before the primary was held in Asheville, and featured the only appearance by Elbie Powers in a debate.<ref>[http://www.citizen-times.com/apps/pbcs.dll/article?AID=200880502004#SlideFrame_19 GOP candidates for governor face off in final debate | CITIZEN-TIMES.com | Asheville Citizen-Times<!-- Bot generated title -->]{{Dead link|date=April 2023 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}</ref>

