===Campaign===
Early in 2008, Libertarian nominee Munger called Perdue a "[[Stepford Wife]]" and said the Republican nominees were "circus clowns."<ref>{{cite news
 |title       = Libertarian Duke professor wants to be N.C. governor
 |work        = [[Raleigh News and Observer]]
 |date        = 2008-02-14
 |url         = http://www.newsobserver.com/politics/story/943804.html
 |access-date  = 2018-12-19
 |archive-url  = https://archive.today/20130814223603/http://web.archive.org/web/20080709085051/http://www.newsobserver.com/politics/story/943804.html
 |archive-date = 2013-08-14
 |url-status     = dead
}}</ref> Prior to May 2008, the North Carolina Libertarian Party and Munger gathered 100,000 signatures of voters in order to qualify to appear on North Carolina's ballot. They, along with the [[Green Party (United States)|Green Party]], sued the state unsuccessfully over the ballot access rules. Munger appeared as one of two keynote speakers at the national Libertarian convention in [[Denver]] in May 2008.<ref name="duke chronicle">{{cite news
 |title=Michael Munger
 |last=Hall
 |first=Christine
 |date=2008-10-29
 |newspaper=[[Duke Chronicle]] 
 |url=https://www.dukechronicle.com/article/2008/10/michael-munger 
 |access-date=2018-12-19 
}}</ref>

When [[Hillary Clinton]] dropped out of the [[United States presidential elections, 2008|2008 presidential election]] ''[[The New York Times]]'' mentioned Perdue as a potential pick for Obama's vice president.<ref name="Zernike2008">{{cite news |first=Kate |last=Zernike |title=She Just Might Be President Someday |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2008/05/18/weekinreview/18zernike.html |work=The New York Times |date=2008-05-18 }}</ref>

Munger called himself "the only [[Liberalism (US)|liberal]] in the race."<ref name="indy weekly">{{cite news
  | title = Statewide Offices
  | work = [[Independent Weekly]]
  | date = 2008-10-22
  | url = http://www.indyweek.com/gyrobase/Content?oid=oid%3A267320
  | access-date = 2008-11-24
  | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20081117165948/http://www.indyweek.com/gyrobase/Content?oid=oid%3A267320
  | archive-date = 2008-11-17
  | url-status = dead
  }}</ref> Munger took more [[Social liberalism|socially liberal]] positions on many issues than Democratic candidate Perdue.  "One reason I haven't been allowed in all the debates is that I'm taking votes from the [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democrats]]. Sixty percent of my supporters are voting for Obama. I'll talk about [[gay marriage]], and Perdue isn't, or doesn't want to."<ref name="reason">{{cite news 
  | title = The Third Man
  | publisher = Reason
  | date = 2008-10-27
  | url = https://www.reason.com/news/show/129671.html
  | access-date = 2008-10-28 }}</ref>  While Democratic candidate Perdue took a hard line on [[illegal immigration]] similar to that of [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]] [[Pat McCrory]], Munger took a position more aligned with [[Barack Obama]].<ref name="wunc">
{{cite news
  | title = The State of Things: Issues Roundup
  | work = [[WUNC (FM)|WUNC]]
  | date = 2008-10-28
  | url = http://wunc.org/tsot/archive/sot1028abc.mp3/view
  | access-date = 2008-10-28
  | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20110716090306/http://wunc.org/tsot/archive/sot1028abc.mp3/view
  | archive-date = 2011-07-16
  | url-status = dead
  }}
</ref>

Perdue raised $15 million and ran attack ads against McCrory, criticizing him for not being tough enough on [[illegal immigration]].<ref name="winston-salem journal">{{cite news|last=Romoser|first=James|date=April 16, 2021|title=Perdue, in a first, edges McCrory|work=[[Winston-Salem Journal]]|publication-date=November 5, 2008|url=https://journalnow.com/perdue-in-a-first-edges-mccrory/article_381b09eb-7177-5312-9dfb-73b8defd5a0e.html|url-status=live|access-date=September 12, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210912103112/https://journalnow.com/perdue-in-a-first-edges-mccrory/article_381b09eb-7177-5312-9dfb-73b8defd5a0e.html|archive-date=September 12, 2021}}</ref>

In October 2008, McCrory received the endorsement of most major newspapers in the state, which typically endorse Democrats.<ref name="daily tarheel2">{{cite news
 |title=McCrory visits Chapel Hill 
 |work=[[Daily Tar Heel]] 
 |date=2008-10-30 
 |url=http://www.dailytarheel.com/news/state_national/mccrory_visits_chapel_hill
 |access-date=2008-11-25 
}}{{dead link|date=December 2016 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}</ref> McCrory's candidacy for governor was endorsed by the ''[[Raleigh News and Observer]]'',<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.newsobserver.com/opinion/editorials/story/1269143.html |title = newsobserver.com {{!}} McCrory's time  |access-date=2013-01-18 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20081029073227/http://www.newsobserver.com/opinion/editorials/story/1269143.html |archive-date=2008-10-29 }}</ref> ''[[The Charlotte Observer]]'',<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.charlotteobserver.com/opinion/story/233637.html |title=We recommend McCrory for governor &#124; CharlotteObserver.com & the Charlotte Observer Newspaper |access-date=2012-05-20 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://archive.today/20120722091204/http://www.charlotteobserver.com/opinion/story/233637.html |archive-date=2012-07-22 }}</ref> the ''[[Greensboro News & Record]]'',<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.news-record.com/content/2008/10/10/article/editorial_endorsement_mccrory_for_governor |title=Editorial endorsement: McCrory for governor : News-Record.com : Greensboro, North Carolina |access-date=2008-11-25 |archive-date=2008-10-15 |archive-url=https://archive.today/20081015140427/http://www.news-record.com/content/2008/10/10/article/editorial_endorsement_mccrory_for_governor |url-status=dead }}</ref> the ''[[Winston-Salem Journal]]'',<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www2.journalnow.com/content/2008/oct/12/election-08-governor/ |title=Election '08: Governor |access-date=2008-11-25 |archive-date=2008-10-15 |archive-url=https://archive.today/20081015032703/http://www2.journalnow.com/content/2008/oct/12/election-08-governor/ |url-status=dead }}</ref> and the [[UNC-Chapel Hill]] ''[[Daily Tar Heel]]''.<ref name="daily tarheel">{{cite news
 |title=McCrory for governor: Charlotte mayor would bring fresh and innovative leadership to Raleigh 
 |work=[[Daily Tar Heel]] 
 |date=2008-10-26 
 |url=http://www.dailytarheel.com/opinion/mccrory_for_governor_charlotte_mayor_would_bring_fresh_and_innovative_leadership_to_raleigh
 |access-date=2008-10-28 
}}{{dead link|date=December 2016 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}</ref>

Perdue received the endorsement of actor and director [[Andy Griffith]], who filmed a campaign ad on her behalf.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://projects.newsobserver.com/under_the_dome/perdues_mayberry_miracle |title=Perdue's Mayberry Miracle? |newspaper=News & Observer |archive-date=20 July 2008 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080720134001/http://projects.newsobserver.com/under_the_dome/perdues_mayberry_miracle |url-status=dead}}</ref>

Perdue defeated McCrory and Munger on November 4, 2008, to win the election.

===Debates===
The first general election debate between Perdue and McCrory was a forum at the North Carolina Bar Association meeting in [[Atlantic Beach, North Carolina|Atlantic Beach]] on June 21.<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://abclocal.go.com/wtvd/story?section=news%2Fstate&id=6219615 |title=ABC 11/Associated Press: Crime among topics at NC gubernatorial debate |access-date=2019-08-28 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110521094932/http://abclocal.go.com/wtvd/story?section=news%2Fstate&id=6219615 |archive-date=2011-05-21 |url-status=dead }}</ref> The first debate between the two that was televised live was conducted by [[WTVD]] on August 19.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://abc11.com/archive/6337624/ |title=ABC11 Eyewitness News' Gubernatorial Debate |access-date=2019-08-28 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080918173931/http://abclocal.go.com/wtvd/story?section=news%2Fpolitics&id=6337624 |archive-date=2008-09-18 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>[http://www.newsobserver.com/politics/story/1185061.html Gov. candidates spar on offshore drilling] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080917001258/http://www.newsobserver.com/politics/story/1185061.html |date=2008-09-17 }}</ref> Another televised debate was held by [[WRAL-TV]] on September 9.<ref>[https://www.wral.com/news/local/politics/story/3516756/ Perdue, McCrory bicker over drilling, vouchers]</ref> Next, McCrory and Perdue met for a debate on education issues at [[SAS Institute]] on September 19. The debate was sponsored by business and education groups and was covered by [[News 14 Carolina]].<ref>[https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mtzwGfaJ-iY YouTube: Everybody's Business Coalition Debate Part 1]</ref><ref>[http://www.newsobserver.com/politics/story/1225622.html News & Observer: McCrory, Perdue offer views on education] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20081226031203/http://www.newsobserver.com/politics/story/1225622.html |date=2008-12-26 }}</ref><ref>[http://news14.com/content/top_stories/599515/gubernatorial-candidates-on-education/Default.aspx News 14: Gubernatorial candidates on education]</ref>

[[Duke University]] professor and [[Libertarian Party (United States)|Libertarian]] candidate [[Michael Munger]] made history as the first third-party candidate to participate in a live, televised gubernatorial debate in North Carolina.<ref name="duke chronicle"/> He made his first debate appearance with McCrory on September 24 at [[UNC-TV]]. Perdue declined to participate in that debate.<ref>[http://www.newsobserver.com/politics/story/1231357.html News & Observer: McCrory, Munger debate; Perdue sits out]{{dead link|date=February 2018 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}</ref>  All three candidates debated for the first time on October 15, in the final debate before the general election.<ref>[http://www.newsobserver.com/politics/story/1256941.html News & Observer: Governor candidates meet in final debate] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20081017125442/http://www.newsobserver.com/politics/story/1256941.html |date=2008-10-17 }}</ref> The hour-long debate, sponsored by [[WTVI]], [[WSOC-TV]] and the [[League of Women Voters]], aired in several television markets.<ref>[http://www.wsoctv.com/politics/17724948/detail.html WSOC: 3 N.C. Governor's Candidates Meet In Last Debate] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110522205334/http://www.wsoctv.com/politics/17724948/detail.html |date=2011-05-22 }}</ref>

Analysts said that McCrory tended to perform better than Perdue in the debates, particularly in "sit-down debates that allowed more back-and-forth between the candidates."<ref>[http://projects.newsobserver.com/under_the_dome/domes_take_perdues_missed_opportunity News & Observer: Dome's Take: Perdue's missed opportunity] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20081023190254/http://projects.newsobserver.com/under_the_dome/domes_take_perdues_missed_opportunity |date=2008-10-23 }}</ref>

